A||||25||85seNUT||Lowering......||||1||
Z||000000||85seNUT||05-26-2001||12:57 PM||||i want to lower my 85 se and i was wondering how much should i do, both for looks and for legal.  and if i do lower it will my tires rub on my wheel well? Thanks<P>Adam<P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Oreif||05-26-2001||02:12 PM||||I lowered mine 1.5". I did not increase the height of my tires but increased the with and I have no rubbing at all. I went from a 215/60R14 to a 225/50R15. The actual diameter of the tire didn't change but the width of the tire did. I don't think there is a law on how low/high the car can be. <P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">    <BR><B><I>Got Emblems?</I></B>||||reg||1
Z||000002||underdog||05-26-2001||03:46 PM||||was that 1.5 inches acheived with the eibach springs on the fierostore page?||||reg||5
Z||000003||Fierowrecker||05-27-2001||10:26 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 85seNUT:<BR><B>i want to lower my 85 se and i was wondering how much should i do, both for looks and for legal.  and if i do lower it will my tires rub on my wheel well? Thanks<P>Adam<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hey Adam!<BR>Try and increase the tire/wheel combo to fill the wheel openings...<BR>Then add a side skirt of hard rubber to be the ground effects...<BR>It will look lower, but actually increase clearance by an inch or two...<BR>The skirting I use is really the rubber fender flare material from pickup trucks...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000004||Oreif||05-27-2001||12:21 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by underdog:<BR><B>was that 1.5 inches acheived with the eibach springs on the fierostore page?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Yes I bought the KTB stage II kit which was the Eibach springs, KYB struts/shocks, and Suspension Tech sway bars with urethane mounts.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||saleenfiero||05-27-2001||12:24 PM||||What would all of the cost to get put in professionally?||||reg||4
Z||000006||Pontiaddict||05-27-2001||05:44 PM||||In most places, the car cannot be lowered so far as any part of the car sticks lower than the rims of the tires. That way if a tire goes flat, you don't (usually) bottom out the car.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Songman||05-27-2001||06:00 PM||||There are laws on how high cars can be in some places.. not sure about how low they can be...<P>Back in my old jacked up hot rod days, the bottom of your rear bumper could be no higher than 31" down in my hometown in GA. You better believe I kept a tape measure with me... I stayed right on it...<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000008||gtcash||05-28-2001||12:33 AM||||Rubber truck flares as fender skirting?<BR>Explain this in more detail please.<BR>Sounds Funky to me<BR>"The Mayor"<P>||||reg||1
Z||000009||MrPBody||05-28-2001||07:59 PM||||I lowered my '85 SE WS6 by cutting the stock springs: 1 coil in front, 1/2 coil in rear.  My biggest lowering-related problem is scraping the spoiler on driveway dips.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||FieroSEt||05-29-2001||08:03 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by MrPBody:<BR><B>I lowered my '85 SE WS6 by cutting the stock springs: 1 coil in front, 1/2 coil in rear.  My biggest lowering-related problem is scraping the spoiler on driveway dips.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>mrPbody...   How much did it lower it cutting the spring like you did???  How hard is it to remove the spring's(front and rear)???  I tried the search function and came up with very little info....  Steve<P>------------------<BR>aka... 1FieroGTRacingInc.<BR>Tyler,Texas<BR>Fiero_87GT@Yahoo.com<BR>(903)445-0849  cell phone||||reg||1
Z||000011||Mr.Frosty||05-29-2001||11:23 AM||||I currently have both rear struts out.I'm trying to put in Suspension Techniques 1.5" drop springs,but I can't get the nuts off the top of the old struts.It's seized on.I have spring compressors,but it wont budge.I've soaked it with a ton of penetrating lube,but nothing.I tried it the uncompressed spring route too "CAREFULLY" as I figured the tension on the top plate would help in breaking it loose,then I could slap on the spring compressors and "Voila"...Nada.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>------------------<BR>85 GT Turbo intercooled||||reg||8
Z||000012||fyerstarter||05-29-2001||11:51 AM||||Mr. Frosty, Save yourself some headache and just go buy some new struts. Struts are cheap and if you want to go lower pick up a set of front struts for a chevy citation and use them for your rears. Be sure and take your old struts with you to make sure they match up.||||reg||1
Z||000013||MrPBody||05-29-2001||02:11 PM||||FieroSEt --<P>I don't know exactly, because I didn't measure before and after.  At the same time, I installed gas-pressurized shocks and struts which <I>raised</I> the car slightly!  I think the net effect of cut springs and gas shocks/struts was to lower the front about 1" and the rear about 1/2".  Ride is also stiffer.<P>Front springs are difficult to remove but easier to install after cutting.  Rear springs require spring compressors but otherwise are not too tough.  (Unless rusty.)<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000014||Jake2m4||05-31-2001||12:24 AM||||fyerstarter - could you please elaborate on the citation struts?  does this work with stock springs? what year citation? etc?<P>Thanks<P>------------------<BR>'85 Fiero Sport Coupe<BR>'95 Pontiac Grand AM SE||||reg||1
Z||000015||sjp777||05-31-2001||01:02 AM||||Don't buy stock no name citation struts. They suck and you'll blow the seals in a year and have to do it all over again.<P>Get the KYB GR-2's. Much better ride and they'll last alot longer. Fierostore has them for $130/pair, but you can probably get them cheaper at an autoparts store, although they might have to order them.<P>The KYB part number is 234005<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/890sm_2.jpg">    <BR>88GT 5sp, in desperate need of an LT1<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/</A>||||reg||1
Z||000016||FieroSEt||05-31-2001||08:17 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by MrPBody:<BR><B>FieroSEt --<P>I don't know exactly, because I didn't measure before and after.  At the same time, I installed gas-pressurized shocks and struts which <I>raised</I> the car slightly!  I think the net effect of cut springs and gas shocks/struts was to lower the front about 1" and the rear about 1/2".  Ride is also stiffer.<P>Front springs are difficult to remove but easier to install after cutting.  Rear springs require spring compressors but otherwise are not too tough.  (Unless rusty.)</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Thanks for the reply Mr Pbody....  Does anyone know of a web page that show's the step's to rempve front and rear spring's???  Thank's ....  Steve||||reg||1
Z||000017||Rob Durham||05-31-2001||12:40 PM||||The easiest and cheapest way is to heat the springs with a torch and watch it drop.<P>I just did mine not more that 20 min. ago and it took me about 45 min. to do it.<P>I'll post some pic's later of the before and after.<P>Rob||||reg||6
Z||000018||saleenfiero||05-31-2001||12:46 PM||||What the hel1 do you mean?  Do you have plastic springs or something?||||reg||1
Z||000019||TRiAD||05-31-2001||12:56 PM||||I hope you guys are only cutting and heating the OLD springs that you're removing. <BR>You don't EVER want to heat or cut the springs on your car.<BR>Also, be VERY careful heating old springs while they're wrapped around a gas strut, it will EXPLODE if you get the strut too hot!<P>FYI.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR><B><I>Looking for a white '88 GT 5 sp...<BR>...Could it be...Found?<BR>Stay tuned!</I></B>||||reg||4
Z||000020||Rob Durham||05-31-2001||04:30 PM||||The springs have to be installed on the car and the weight of the car has to be on all 4 wheels when heating them. <P><BR>Yes you must be careful of the gas struts, but there is more than enough room to get the torch in place and NOT get the flame near the struts.<P><BR>You take a torch (have a hose with the water running for cooling the springs) and heat the lower coil of the spring, the heat and weight of the engine you can literaly watch the car drop, on the front springs you need to have someone (250lbs or heavier) sit on the fender of the side you are working on as there is not enough weight on the front of the car.<P><BR>You can drop the car 1/4 inch at a time so you can see how it looks <P><BR>Any one that works on street rods can help you out. I've also done this to 3 P/T Cruisers and it looks damn nice!<P><BR>This is an afordable way to lower your Fiero without any sacrafice in the performance of your springs.<P><BR>Rob<p>[This message has been edited by Rob Durham (edited 05-31-2001).]||||reg||6
Z||000021||Rob Durham||05-31-2001||07:57 PM||||Here are some pic's<P> <IMG SRC="http://rs-designs.com/Fiero/Group1.jpg"> <P>Rob||||reg||1
Z||000022||danny boy||05-31-2001||10:05 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mr.Frosty:<BR><B>  I can't get the nuts off the top of the old struts.It's seized on. <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P> Hold the very top of the strut rod(under the mounting plate) with a pipe wrench or locking pliers, and turn the nut with a wrench. or find the correct size socket for the end of the strut rod, put it on, use a 1/2" drive deepwell socket for the nut, slide an extension through the big socket, hold the rod in place and turn the big socket with the pipe wrench. The big socket I use has a hex head like a sparkplug socket.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000023||FLASHY||05-31-2001||11:15 PM||||I ended up just cutting my coils. The ride was only a tad stiffer but no big deal. I like it that way anyway because it helps hug the road on turns (not to mention it's lower center of gravity helps too). The only thing I have to watch out for is when the car is jacked off the ground the rear coils fall out of the strut sockets. You have to lower the car slowly while making sure they are seated properly. It's worth it for me. Here's a pic of the difference...<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/steve_lowered.jpg"> <P>Expect to get a wheel alignment after though. It most likely will go out of wack.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000024||Slammed Fiero||06-01-2001||12:31 AM||||Lowering a car with a torch is an invitation for problems. Hardening methods for springs can be affected and weaken the spring , you weaken the spring it makes it a different rate (lighter), a fiero does not need lighter springs , you will only exagerate things like trailing throttle oversteer. Cutting is better , but also kind of iffy. you increase the spring rate when you cut it. Also I'm not sure what style stock fiero springs are , but if they are progressive rate (as a lot of cars are) you can cause huge Problems.<P>Discount tire direct sells Suspension Tech lowering springs for $188 . If you want a proper lowering job , spend the money to get it done ,it's much safer...<P>no flames intended.<P><P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1607505&a=12259132&p=48230089&Sequence=0&res=high">     <BR>Jonathan McCreery<BR>86 GT 4spd<BR>Koni's , Coilovers, Big Bars , 17" Trmotorsports Typhoons , 11.25" vented discs, Wilwood 4 Piston Calipers , Pirelli P Zero's, Short Shift, No cat , Cold air induction , Geni Stainless tips, White face gauges , Momo shift , momo pedals..Autocrossed , Tracked , abused.||||reg||1
Z||000025||Mr.Frosty||06-01-2001||11:46 AM||||Hey Slammed Fiero,<BR>   I can't get over how cool your car looks lowered in your signiture.Those coilovers,how much did they lower the rear?It looks pretty low.I like it.That's the height I want mine to sit.I think I'm going to get Koni adjustable struts to get the desired height.<P>------------------<BR>85 GT Turbo intercooled||||reg||10
